/-!
# `Work.Wire123.Defs` — the vocabulary of the wiring area for Theorem 1.2 (3)

This module is the vocabulary of the **wiring of Theorem 1.2 (3) of the challenge** (the `n ≥ 5`
case over an infinite field), i.e. of the passage

```
  the CY3 L-link of `thm:CY3`  →  `Cr 𝕜 5` is not generated by pseudo-regularizable elements
  (Submission/Work/CY3, Submission/Work/CY3Stab)          →  `Cr 𝕜 n`, `n ≥ 5`
```

into `Submission.MotivicInvariants.theorem_1_2_3`.

## Why this module exists: the two interfaces of the delivered chain are not instantiable

The delivered `n ≥ 5` chain is `Work.CY3Stab.theorem_1_2_3_of_G2 hn 𝕜 M C T`, whose three
inputs are

* `M : Work.G2.CY3DatumZ` — the `ℤ`-model of `thm:CY3` (no producer exists: the model is a
  structure of named geometric inputs, and the `G₂` group scheme, Bertini, blow-ups and
  intersection theory are not in Mathlib);
* `C : Work.G2.CubicInput 𝕜` — the "cubic self-intersection is an isomorphism invariant" input;
* `T : Work.CY3.TotaroInput 𝕜` — Totaro's `thm:BurtCY`/`cor-CYnSB`.

Two of these are not merely *unproduced* but **uninstantiable**:

* `Work.G2.CubicInput 𝕜` in its originally delivered (predicate) shape is **refutable**
  (`Work.Wire123.not_legacyCubicInput`); the repaired shape of `Submission/Work/G2/Defs.lean`
  carries the function and is inhabited, and the per-pair/`CubicInvariant` interface below is the
  consistent one: its field `unique`
  asks that the class `Work.G2.CubicSelfIntersection 𝕜 X n` determine `n`, but that class has no
  fields, so it is inhabited for *every* `n`; `unique` then yields `0 = 1`.  The obstruction is
  not in the geometry — it is that a *named input asserting the well-definedness of a function
  must supply the function*, not merely a predicate.
* `Work.CY3.TotaroInput 𝕜` (and `Work.K3.TotaroInput 𝕜`) quantifies over *all* pairs of
  `𝕜`-varieties, with the geometric hypotheses carried by the **fieldless** predicates
  `Work.CY3.IsKTrivial`, `Work.CY3.PicardRankOne` — which are therefore automatically satisfied.
  The statement it makes is thus "any two non-isomorphic `𝕜`-varieties are not stably
  birational", which is *false* (e.g. `𝔸¹` and `ℙ¹` are birational, hence stably birational, and
  not isomorphic).  Again the repair is to *localise the quantification*: Totaro's theorem says
  something about the two centres at hand, not about all pairs.

This module supplies the **repaired, consistent** interfaces

* `Work.Wire123.CubicInvariant 𝕜` — a *function* `cubic X` with `cubic X = cubic Y` for
  `X ≅ Y`, from which `lem-nSBG2`'s `¬ IsoK` follows by `14 ≠ 42`
  (`Work.Wire123.CubicInvariant.notIso`);
* `Work.Wire123.TotaroInputAt 𝕜 X Y` — Totaro's theorem *at a pair*: at the two centres of the
  link, `¬ IsoK ⟹ ¬ SBK`;
* `Work.Wire123.CentreSeparation 𝕜 D` — the form in which the chain actually consumes Totaro's
  theorem: the two centres of a CY3 datum are not stably birational.  This is the weakest
  hypothesis of the whole `n ≥ 5` chain, it is *consistent* (the paper's `thm:CY3` + `lem-nSBG2`
  produce it), and it is enough (`Submission/Work/Wire123/Chain.lean`).

Everything here is `sorry`-free and axiom-free; see `Submission/Work/Wire123/NOTES.md`.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false
set_option linter.style.haveILetI false
attribute [instance] MotivicInvariants.Variety.over MotivicInvariants.Variety.isIntegral

namespace Work.Wire123

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits AlgebraicGeometry AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
  MotivicInvariants Order

noncomputable section

variable {𝕜 : Type} [Field 𝕜]

/-- Local name for `Work.CY3.IsoOver` (isomorphisms over the base field). -/
abbrev IsoK (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] (X Y : Scheme.{0}) [X.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))]
    [Y.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] : Prop :=
  Work.CY3.IsoOver 𝕜 X Y

/-- Local name for `Work.CY3.StablyBirational`. -/
abbrev SBK (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] (X Y : Scheme.{0}) [Variety 𝕜 X] [Variety 𝕜 Y] : Prop :=
  Work.CY3.StablyBirational 𝕜 X Y

/-! ## 1. The repaired cubic interface -/

/-- **The corrected form of `Work.G2.CubicInput`.**

Mathematically, the input of `lem-nSBG2` is: *there is a function* `X ↦ c₁(M|_X)³` *on
`𝕜`-varieties, invariant under isomorphisms over `𝕜`*, together with the two computed values
`c₁(M|_Z)³ = 14`, `c₁(M'|_{Z'})³ = 42` at the two centres.  The two structural facts are exactly
`cubic` and `cubic_iso` below; the two values are hypotheses *about this function at the two
centres* (`hZ`, `hZ'` of `Work.Wire123.CubicInvariant.notIso`), because they are computations in
the intersection theory of the individual varieties.

Contrast `Work.G2.CubicSelfIntersection 𝕜 X n` + `Work.G2.CubicInput 𝕜`: the predicate
`CubicSelfIntersection` has no fields, hence is inhabited for every `n`, so the "well-definedness"
clause of `CubicInput` is `n = m` for arbitrary `n, m : ℤ` — a contradiction
(`Work.Wire123.not_legacyCubicInput`).  The function `cubic` *is* the content that was missing. -/
structure CubicInvariant (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] where
  /-- the cubic self-intersection `c₁(M|_X)³` of the ample generator of `NS(X)`, as a function of
  the variety -/
  cubic : (X : Scheme.{0}) → [Variety 𝕜 X] → ℤ
  /-- `c₁(M)³` is invariant under isomorphisms over `𝕜` (an isomorphism carries the ample
  generator to the ample generator, and intersection numbers are invariant) -/
  cubic_iso : ∀ {X Y : Scheme.{0}} [Variety 𝕜 X] [Variety 𝕜 Y],
    IsoK 𝕜 X Y → cubic X = cubic Y

/-- **`lem-nSBG2`, first half, with the interface repaired**: two `𝕜`-varieties whose cubic
self-intersections are the distinct numbers `14` and `42` are not isomorphic over `𝕜`.

This is `Work.G2.not_isoOver_of_cubic` with `Work.G2.CubicInput` replaced by the consistent
`Work.Wire123.CubicInvariant` (and its two value hypotheses made explicit).  The arithmetic
`14 ≠ 42` does the work, exactly as in the paper. -/
theorem CubicInvariant.notIso (C : CubicInvariant 𝕜) {X Y : Scheme.{0}} [Variety 𝕜 X]
    [Variety 𝕜 Y] (hX : C.cubic X = 14) (hY : C.cubic Y = 42) : ¬ IsoK 𝕜 X Y :=
  fun h => (by norm_num : (14 : ℤ) ≠ 42) (hX.symm.trans ((C.cubic_iso h).trans hY))

/-- **The repaired `G₂` interface and `CubicInvariant` are the same notion**: `Work.G2.CubicInput 𝕜`
now carries the function `X ↦ c₁(M|_X)³` together with its isomorphism-invariance (the repair landed
in `G2/Defs.lean`, see `Submission/Work/Wire123/NOTES.md`), so the two convert into each other
trivially. -/
def CubicInvariant.toCubicInput (C : CubicInvariant 𝕜) : Work.G2.CubicInput 𝕜 :=
  ⟨C.cubic, C.cubic_iso⟩

/-- Conversely, the (repaired) `Work.G2.CubicInput` is a `Work.Wire123.CubicInvariant`. -/
def CubicInvariant.ofCubicInput (C : Work.G2.CubicInput 𝕜) : CubicInvariant 𝕜 :=
  ⟨C.cubic, C.cubic_iso⟩

/-- **The repaired `lem-nSBG2`, first half, at a `ℤ`-model**: if the function `cubic` takes the two
values `14`, `42` at the two centres of the model, they are not isomorphic. -/
theorem CubicInvariant.notIso_centres (C : CubicInvariant 𝕜) (M : Work.G2.CY3DatumZ)
    (hZ : C.cubic (Work.G2.baseChangeZ 𝕜 M.ZZ) = 14)
    (hZ' : C.cubic (Work.G2.baseChangeZ 𝕜 M.ZZ') = 42) :
    ¬ Work.CY3.IsoOver 𝕜 (Work.G2.baseChangeZ 𝕜 M.ZZ) (Work.G2.baseChangeZ 𝕜 M.ZZ') :=
  C.notIso hZ hZ'

/-- The same, with the two values transported along an isomorphism instead of assumed at `X`, `Y`
directly: the form in which a producer (which knows the values at *its* models of the two centres)
supplies them. -/
theorem CubicInvariant.notIso_of_iso {C : CubicInvariant 𝕜} {X Y X' Y' : Scheme.{0}}
    [Variety 𝕜 X] [Variety 𝕜 Y] [Variety 𝕜 X'] [Variety 𝕜 Y']
    (hX : IsoK 𝕜 X X') (hY : IsoK 𝕜 Y Y') (hX' : C.cubic X' = 14) (hY' : C.cubic Y' = 42) :
    ¬ IsoK 𝕜 X Y :=
  fun h => C.notIso hX' hY'
    (Work.CY3.IsoOver.trans (Work.CY3.IsoOver.symm hX) (Work.CY3.IsoOver.trans h hY))

/-! ## 2. The repaired Totaro interface -/

/-- **Totaro's theorem at a pair** (`thm:BurtCY`, `cor-CYnSB`): for two `K`-trivial varieties of
Picard rank one which are not isomorphic, stable birationality fails.

This is the *localised* form of the named input `Work.CY3.TotaroInput 𝕜`: the universal form
quantifies over all pairs of `𝕜`-varieties, its geometric hypotheses (`Work.CY3.IsKTrivial`,
`Work.CY3.PicardRankOne`) are fieldless classes and hence automatically satisfied, so it asserts
"non-isomorphic varieties are not stably birational" — which is false
(`Work.Wire123.not_totaroInput`).  At a *pair* (the pair of centres of a link) the statement is
exactly the paper's corollary and is consistent.

**This is now the in-place interface of the CY3 chapter** (`Work.CY3.TotaroInputAt`, added there
additively next to the universal form, with the conversion `Work.CY3.TotaroInput.toInputAt`); the
name below is an abbreviation of it, so the two are the same notion. -/
abbrev TotaroInputAt (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] (X Y : Scheme.{0}) [Variety 𝕜 X]
    [Variety 𝕜 Y] : Prop :=
  Work.CY3.TotaroInputAt 𝕜 X Y

/-- The universal named input `Work.CY3.TotaroInput` gives the pair form at every pair satisfying
its (named) hypotheses — this is the direction in which the two interfaces are compared, and it
shows that `TotaroInputAt` is the weaker (and consistent) statement. -/
theorem TotaroInputAt.of_totaroInput (T : Work.CY3.TotaroInput 𝕜) {X Y : Scheme.{0}}
    [Variety 𝕜 X] [Variety 𝕜 Y] (hX : Work.CY3.IsKTrivial 𝕜 X)
    (hY : Work.CY3.IsKTrivial 𝕜 Y) (pX : Work.CY3.PicardRankOne 𝕜 X)
    (pY : Work.CY3.PicardRankOne 𝕜 Y) : TotaroInputAt 𝕜 X Y :=
  Work.CY3.TotaroInput.toInputAt 𝕜 T hX hY pX pY

/-! ## 3. The residual of the `n ≥ 5` chain -/

/-- **The residue of Totaro's theorem at the two centres of a CY3 datum**: `Z` and `Z'` are not
stably birational.  This is the *only* thing the whole `n ≥ 5` chain needs beyond the geometric
datum `Work.CY3.CY3Data 𝕜` itself (`Submission/Work/Wire123/Chain.lean`), and it is exactly what
the paper's `lem-nSBG2` (via `cor-CYnSB` and `14 ≠ 42`) provides for the two `K`-trivial
threefolds of Picard rank one.

The two centres are written in the order in which the counting produces them
(`Work.CY3Stab.Prod.stablyBirational_cyl_two`), i.e. `Z'` first. -/
def CentreSeparation (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] (D : Work.CY3.CY3Data 𝕜) : Prop :=
  ¬ Work.CY3.StablyBirational 𝕜 D.Z' D.Z

/-- The same in the order `(Z, Z')`. -/
def CentreSeparation' (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] (D : Work.CY3.CY3Data 𝕜) : Prop :=
  ¬ Work.CY3.StablyBirational 𝕜 D.Z D.Z'

theorem centreSeparation_iff (D : Work.CY3.CY3Data 𝕜) :
    CentreSeparation 𝕜 D ↔ CentreSeparation' 𝕜 D :=
  ⟨fun h hsb => h (Work.Totaro.stablyBirational_symm hsb),
    fun h hsb => h (Work.Totaro.stablyBirational_symm hsb)⟩

/-- **The residual of Theorem 1.2 (3) for `n ≥ 5`**: a CY3 L-link data whose two centres are not
stably birational.

This is the paper's `thm:CY3` (the `G₂`-Grassmannian L-link with its two `K`-trivial threefolds of
Picard rank one, `Work.CY3.CY3Data`) together with the conclusion of `cor-CYnSB` at the two
centres.  It is the *minimal* mathematics that the `n ≥ 5` case still owes; every formal step from
it to `¬ GenByPseudoRegularizable 𝕜 n` is proved in `Submission/Work/Wire123/Chain.lean`. -/
def CY3Residual (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] : Prop :=
  ∃ (D : Work.CY3.CY3Data 𝕜), CentreSeparation 𝕜 D

end

end Work.Wire123
